{'global_id': 'medeasi_000112', 'source_text': 'Crohn disease should be suspected in a patient with inflammatory or obstructive symptoms or in a patient without prominent GI symptoms but with perianal fistulas or abscesses or with otherwise unexplained arthritis, erythema nodosum, fever, anemia, or (in a child) stunted growth. A family history of Crohn disease also increases the index of suspicion.', 'source_metrics': {'FRE': 18.69, 'ARI': 18.3, 'FKGL': 17.4, 'Dale-Chall': 11.41, 'char_count': 353, 'alphanum_count': 292, 'word_count': 62, 'sentence_count': 2}, 'metadata': {'dataset': 'medeasi', 'experiment_split': '', 'domain': 'medical', 'language': 'en', 'annotation': 'human', 'level': 'sentence'}, 'simplification_text': 'A doctor may suspect Crohn disease in a person with recurring crampy abdominal pain and diarrhea, particularly if the person has a family history of Crohn disease or a history of problems around the anus.', 'target_metrics': {'FRE': 19.03, 'ARI': 19.0, 'FKGL': 19.3, 'Dale-Chall': 9.88, 'char_count': 204, 'alphanum_count': 168, 'word_count': 37, 'sentence_count': 1, 'char_compression_rate': 0.58, 'word_compression_rate': 0.6, 'sentence_compression_rate': 0.5, 'BLEU': 7.235003364388359, 'BERTScore': 0.8661102652549744}, 'simplification_version': 1, 'grade_level': -1, 'simplification_dimensions': {'linguistic': {'lexical': False, 'syntactic': False}}}
